<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Robots on Earth and in Space</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Future Exploration of Space</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Evaluate spoken advertisements for bias and intent. Interactive: Participate in discussions on future innovations.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can evaluate spoken advertisements about robotics in Panama for persuasive language, identifying bias (e.g., "The ad from the robotics fair uses terms like 'cutting-edge' and 'essential' to highlight how Panama's agricultural industry is adopting robotic technology to automate processes.")
            Can participate in discussions on future tech, noting opinions (e.g., "The speaker supports the use of robots in space but doesn't mention any risks, like technical failures or ethical concerns.")
            Can ask questions about the intent behind tech innovations (e.g., "What does the ad suggest about AI's impact on daily life?" "The ad suggests that AI will make daily tasks more efficient and ignores risks such as job displacement or privacy concerns.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a captivating image of a futuristic factory with robots and astronauts exploring a distant planet. Students are asked to share initial thoughts and feelings about the images. Teacher writes keywords on the board. (e.g., <b>robot</b>, <b>space</b>, <b>automation</b>, <b>exploration</b>, <b>machine</b>, <b>planet</b>, <b>mission</b>). Students discuss in pairs: "What do you think the future of work on Earth and space exploration will look like with robots?" (Pragmatic: Participating in discussions on the use of robotics in various fields).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the warm-up and the upcoming listening task: /r/ in "<b>robots</b>" and /s/ in "<b>space</b>", "<b>automation</b>", "<b>efficient</b>", "<b>explore</b>", "<b>industry</b>", "<b>technology</b>". Teacher exaggerates mouth movements and repeats words clearly. The teacher also models correct pronunciation of '-ed' endings for past participles that will be relevant later, e.g., "controlled" (/d/), "launched" (/t/), "operated" (/ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms by asking:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <b>robot</b> usually a human or a machine?" (Expected: A machine.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a task is <b>automated</b>, does a person do it manually?" (Expected: No, a machine does it.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio recording of a 1-1.5 minute spoken advertisement for a fictional "Panama Robotics & Space Exploration Expo," promoting robotic applications in agriculture and future space missions. The ad uses persuasive language and includes phrases with future perfect and passive voice. (Genre: Advertisement, Register: Persuasive/Informative, Format: Audio).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The ability to identify persuasive language, bias, and intent in spoken advertisements related to technology and future innovations. (Listening: Receptive: Evaluate spoken advertisements for bias and intent).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen to the advertisement for the first time and answer a general question: "What is being advertised and what are the two main areas where robots are highlighted?" (Expected: Panama Robotics & Space Exploration Expo; agriculture and space missions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and mark True or False for statements based on the ad's content. Example statements: "Robots will only assist in farming," "By 2040, new planets will have been discovered by automated rovers." (Teacher uses passive voice and future perfect here, linking to grammar focus).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Information Matching):</b> Students listen for specific details, matching the advertised benefits of robotics (e.g., "increased yield," "safer <b>exploration</b>") with the relevant field (e.g., <b>agriculture</b>, <b>space</b>).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>Accuracy focus on Listening</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Identifying key vocabulary (e.g., <b>automation</b>, <b>industry</b>, <b>collect</b>, <b>launch</b>, <b>mission</b>) and recognizing grammar structures like future perfect for predictions (e.g., "By 2030, AI <b>will have transformed</b> industries") and passive voice for scientific reporting (e.g., "Data <b>is collected</b> by <b>robots</b> for analysis").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a transcript of the ad with 8-10 target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>technology</b>, <b>automation</b>, <b>agriculture</b>, <b>explore</b>, <b>launch</b>, <b>satellite</b>, <b>mission</b>, <b>efficient</b>) removed. They listen to the ad again and fill in the blanks. Teacher checks answers as a class, reinforcing pronunciation of /r/ and /s/ words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Grammar Spotting & Discussion):</b> Students listen for a fourth time, specifically noting sentences that use the future perfect and passive voice. On a worksheet, they identify and underline these sentences. Then, in pairs, they discuss the effect of these grammatical structures. For example: "Why does the ad say 'new techniques <b>will have been developed</b>' instead of 'we will develop new techniques'?" (Highlighting the passive voice for scientific reporting/focus on result). "What impact <b>will automation have had</b> on the agricultural <b>industry</b> by 2030, according to the ad?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Identifying Persuasion):</b> Students discuss in pairs specific phrases from the ad that sound persuasive or biased (e.g., "cutting-edge," "essential for success," "limitless possibilities"). They are encouraged to use some of the new vocabulary and the zero conditional (e.g., "If an ad uses phrases like 'essential,' it implies there's no other good choice."). They note down one example of persuasive language and explain why it might be biased.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group discussion (3-4 students) followed by a brief whole-class share-out.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students listen to a short (1-minute) audio clip from a fictional 'tech news report' featuring two experts discussing the future of robotics in space <b>exploration</b>. One expert is highly enthusiastic, emphasizing benefits like discovering new planets and <b>collecting</b> data efficiently. The other expert is more cautious, mentioning risks like technical failures and ethical concerns regarding <b>control</b> and <b>safety</b>. (Theme: The Future Exploration of Space, Scenario: Robots on Earth and in Space).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In their groups, students discuss the opinions presented by each speaker. They must:
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identify the main points and opinions of each speaker.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evaluate if either speaker shows bias or leaves out important information. (e.g., "The speaker supports the use of <b>robots</b> in <b>space</b> but doesn't mention any risks, like technical failures or ethical concerns.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask questions about the intent behind the innovations or opinions discussed. (e.g., "What does the ad suggest about AI's impact on daily life?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Offer potential solutions or balanced perspectives, ensuring all viewpoints are considered (Sociolinguistic). They should try to use future perfect (e.g., "By 2050, <b>robots will have made</b> space travel safer") or passive voice (e.g., "New <b>equipment is being designed</b> for outer <b>orbit</b>").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Teacher monitors, encourages use of target vocabulary, grammar, and respectful dialogue (Pragmatic: Encouraging respectful dialogue on diverse perspectives regarding the impacts of robotics).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>Formative assessment and self-reflection</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group discussion, focusing on listening for bias and participation in discussion.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students collectively provide one observation about bias/intent from the tech news report discussion and ask one question to the class about the future of robotics. (e.g., "We think the first expert was biased because she didn't mention the challenges of robot <b>repair</b>. Our question is: If <b>robots are programmed</b> for difficult tasks, what happens if they malfunction?")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., <b>robot</b>, <b>space</b>, <b>automated</b>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to identify persuasive language/bias in spoken input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in group discussion, noting opinions and asking relevant questions about intent.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempted use of target vocabulary and grammar (future perfect, passive voice, zero conditional).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Name one new vocabulary word you learned today and explain its meaning."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel about identifying bias in spoken information about technology?" (Scale of 1-5).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b><br>Review and forward planning</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about listening for bias today, and what helped you the most?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can identifying bias in advertisements or news reports be useful in your daily life?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What worked well in helping students evaluate spoken advertisements for bias and intent?" (e.g., The multiple listening tasks, the specific questions targeting persuasive language).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson, especially regarding vocabulary retention or deeper analysis of intent?" (e.g., More visual aids for abstract vocabulary, specific sentence starters for expressing opinions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 2, students will read a text about robotics in space exploration. The vocabulary and concepts introduced today through listening will be reinforced, allowing them to apply their understanding of potential biases and future impacts to a written context. They will encounter similar vocabulary (<b>robot</b>, <b>technology</b>, <b>space</b>, <b>exploration</b>, <b>mission</b>) and grammatical structures (passive voice, future perfect) in the reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>